{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the people saw that Moses delayed to come down from the mountain.</b> This was on the 16th of Tammuz. How so? The Torah was given on the 6th of Sivan, and all six days the cloud covered the mountain — this is what is written (Exodus 24:16): ״ויכסהו הענן ששת ימים״ — and on that very day, the sixth, the Torah was given, and on the next day he went up, as it says (ibid.): ״ויקרא אל משה ביום השביעי״ — and he remained there forty days, which is what is written immediately after: ״ויהי משה בהר ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה״. When do they end? On the 17th of Tammuz, for he had told them: at the end of forty days I will come at six hours of the day. But they erred in the reckoning, counting the seventh day on which he went up as part of the tally, and so they reckoned that they would end on the 16th of Tammuz. This is the meaning of ״וירא העם כי בושש משה״ — they saw that six hours of the day had arrived and Moses had not come, and they thought he had been detained there and would not come again." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash that the Satan brought darkness and confusion upon the world, and showed Israel a vision of his bier. Therefore they said, ״קום עשה לנו אלהים״. But Moses had told them: forty full days — and the seventh day on which he went up is not counted in the tally — and thus they would end on the 17th of Tammuz. This is the meaning of ״וישכימו ממחרת״ — the morrow of the 16th, which is the 17th of Tammuz, on which they offered the calf sacrifices, and on that same day at six hours Moses came and broke the tablets. This is what the Rabbis of blessed memory received: on the 17th of Tammuz the tablets were broken; on the morrow, the 18th of Tammuz, he burned the calf and judged its worshippers and commanded the sons of Levi to slay them; on the morrow, the 19th, he went up to Elohim and remained there forty days in prayer, supplicating and praying to atone for them for the sin of the calf — this is what is written in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9:25): ״את ארבעים היום ואת ארבעים הלילה אשר התנפלתי״. When did they end? On the new moon of Elul. And on the new moon of Elul he went up to the mountain, as he was told (Exodus 34:2): ״ועלית בבקר אל הר סיני״ — and he remained there another forty days to receive the second tablets. When did they end? On the Day of Atonement; on it that sin was atoned for him, and he brought down in his hand the second tablets, and it was a day of pardon and atonement. On the morrow of the Day of Atonement they began with the donation for the Tabernacle, and it was erected on the first of Nisan. From this you learn that the sin of the calf preceded the section of כי תשא, and according to the Midrash of the Rabbis of blessed memory, ״ונתנו איש כופר נפשו״ — which came to atone for the sin of the calf — there is no earlier and later in the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:15.157782688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}